Title: "Overview"
Id: "4d980f35-73f3-4400-a0d0-ae79e2a7db57"

Text: "The definition-like top-level groups are `Declares:`, `Defines:`, `Realizes:`,
`Refines:`, and `States:`. Each is backed by a command heading, introduces that command's
signature, and may carry the support sections shown below.

```mlg-fragment
Requires?: <RequiresItemUnion>+
Enables?: <EnablesItemUnion>+
Documented?: <DocumentedItemUnion>+
Justification?: <HaveGroup>+
Aliases?: <AliasItemUnion>+
Writing?: \"<WritingAlias>\"+
References?: <ResourceHeader>+
Metadata?: <MetadataItemUnion>+
```

Which one to use:

- `Declares:` introduces a reusable type, interface, relation shape, or
  statement shape. It supports `extends:`, `specifies:`, and `satisfies:`, and
  is the only kind that may use an infix-specification heading
  (`[A \:subset:/ B]`).
- `Defines:` introduces a concrete typed value or operation. It may be marked
  `abstractly:` and supports value-supplying `specifies:` plus `expresses:`.
- `Realizes:` supplies every component left open by an abstract `Defines:` item.
- `Refines:` defines a refined command in terms of another command. It is the
  only kind that may use a refined command header (`::`).
- `States:` defines a named statement with a required `that:` body.

`Declares:`, `Defines:`, `Realizes:`, and `States:` must include at least one of `called:` or
`written:` in `Documented:`. `Refines:` must instead include `adjective:`, and
its `Documented:` rejects `called:`.

`Requires:` is used for definitional capabilities or required definitions,
while `Enables:` is used for additional capabilities, build-backed forms, and
views. For type checking, ordinary capabilities of both are combined. A
`Requires:` `definition:` item refers to a top-level `Defines:` item.

`Justification:` replaces the older `Justified:` section, and every non-prose
top-level item may carry its own `Writing:` section after `Aliases:`."
Id: "1764c7bb-3657-4005-9490-4847e48be174"
